From 4a207402e5e01a3378235f2042211e1b85918f14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jared Wyles <jared.wyles@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 21:05:00 +1100
Subject: [PATCH 18/32] skippy: open models without rollback snapshots unless a
 plan needs them

Restores three changes James Dumay made on the previous pin that the
rebuilt queue lost.

Ordinary decoding keeps no recurrent rollback snapshots. A caller that
passes recurrent_rollback_tokens = 0, as serving does when no speculative
plan is attached, now gets n_rs_seq = 0; the native side had forced at
least 2, so every lane of a recurrent or hybrid model kept and refreshed two
extra copies of its recurrent state. skippy_runtime_config_default sets the
depth to 2 for C callers that want the old behaviour, and SKIPPY_RS_SEQ may
now name 0.

Backend samplers serve one output row per sequence
(n_outputs_max_per_seq = 1): upstream builds a sampler chain per row it may
have to sample, which otherwise means one per row of the whole batch. Only
sequences with an attached backend sampler are held to it, so verify windows
and raw readouts keep their multi-row outputs. The MTP sidecar consumes raw
rows and keeps no cap.

skippy_decode_batch_sampled rejects a batch that names a session twice
before it touches any verification window, instead of decoding two tokens
into one lane.

skippy-backend-sample-capacity and skippy-recurrent-retention cover the
output cap and explicit snapshot retention on dense and hybrid fixtures.

No ABI change: skippy_runtime_config keeps its layout.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01QA3dWey3RBtBgDz1Tery9Y
